    stem: "Consider a variation of the game described in part (a) with two key differences: the matchsticks are initially arranged into two separate piles on the board and a player can remove any number of matchsticks from a single pile on their go. We will call the two piles pile X and pile Y. For example, in a game where pile X contains 10 matchsticks and pile Y contains 9 matchsticks, a player may decide to remove 9 matchsticks from pile Y on their go. The player who removes the last matchstick(s) wins the game.",
    questionText: "Describe a winning move from state (2, 3).",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal"}, totalMarks: 2,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "A winning move for player A from state (2, 3) would be AY1.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-2", kind: "point", verbatim: "This move leaves the opponent – player B - in state (2,2).", marks: null },
    { id: "r-3", kind: "point", verbatim: "From (2,2) B can BX1 → (1,2) certain victory for A (as per part (ii)).", marks: null },
    { id: "r-4", kind: "point", verbatim: "From (2,2) B can BY1 → (2,1) also certain victory for A.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-5", kind: "point", verbatim: "From (2,2) B can also BX2 → (0,2) or BY2 → (2,0) both certain victories for A.", marks: null },
    ],
